<onlyinclude>'''Neural processing unit''' ('''NPU''') denotes a specialized processor or accelerator designed to execute neural-network operators efficiently, commonly through parallel multiply–accumulate (MAC) engines and a memory hierarchy arranged for tensor reuse. The term describes a functional class, not one uniform instruction set, chip configuration, or guaranteed performance level.</onlyinclude>

== Overview ==
An NPU commonly executes convolution, matrix multiplication, activation, and other supported tensor operations. A host processor typically handles control flow and delegates compatible model subgraphs to the accelerator. [[Computer memory|Memory]] movement, operator coverage, tensor dimensions, and compiler mapping determine realized performance, not merely peak arithmetic throughput.

